### Layout
The application is called `$app_identifier` throughout this project. That name is
used for the role in `roles/$app_identifier`, the inventory groups
`$app_identifier`, `${app_identifier}_stg` and `${app_identifier}_prd`, the matching files
in `group_vars`, the nginx site and the deployment directory under `/var/www`.

The role's own variables stay under the `app` key regardless of that name, so
`app.domain` and `app.config` are the same in every generated project.

### Environment variables
Values are taken from `app.config` dictionary, and are transformed using a filter plugin.

As a result, this configuration...

```yaml
app:
  config:
    redis:
      host: 127.0.0.1
      port: 6379
```

...produces the following lines in the .env-file:

```
REDIS_HOST: 127.0.0.1
REDIS_PORT: 6379
```

The key of the respective environment variable is constructed from all the parent and child keys relative to the `app.config` dictionary, separated by an underscore, and written in all-caps.

In the above example, `redis.host` becomes `REDIS_HOST`, and `redis.port` becomes `REDIS_PORT`.

The dictionary keys can be nested infinitely, and the filter plugin also has no effect on variables that already contain underscores.

### Deployments
This boilerplate ships without a CI configuration. Add the pipeline your
organization uses, and have it run:

```sh
ansible-playbook $playbook_filename --limit production
```
